  "query_preview": "..Drier air (in the mid-levels) has moved into the \nmajority of the area behind yesterday's s/wv. However, PW values are \nstill well above normal, boundary layer dew points remain high and \nshear has increased. After the small amount of suppression from the \nsubsidence and drying this morning, we do expect another active \nafternoon. Coverage will not be as widespread, without the forcing \nfrom the s/wv, but the 18Z KFGZ sounding shows potential for some \nsevere weather and flooding from terrain-locked and training storms.\n\nFor Sunday and Monday, additional moisture is expected to move north \nas a compact low pressure system moves through the northern states. \nWe expect more of the same weather, but potentially greater coverage \nand organization, due to the return of synoptic-dscale forcing.\n\nFor the remainder of next week, a battle between trough off the west \ncoast and high pressure to the east will set up a series of up and \ndown days. Basically, a regime featuring dry air fighting to move in \nfrom the west, while the Monsoonal moisture pool keeps getting \npulled northward. Expect active days to continue as a result, but \nespecially for the eastern and southeastern zones, closer to the \ndeeper moisture.",
